const VK={"Cross Road Blues, by Robert Johnson, 1937.": "9d8aeece5ac3", "Delta blues.": "564e12afef14", "Johnson was a Mississippi singer and guitarist who recorded only twenty nine songs, and legend says he sold his soul at a crossroads to play so well.": "b06dacb3a140", "Listen for his bottleneck slide guitar answering his voice, and his free rhythm, where he adds beats and sings in a different rhythm from the one he plays.": "72cc3328a682", "Now playing: Cross Road Blues, by Robert Johnson.": "08ddfcb2245b", "Lost Your Head Blues, by Bessie Smith, 1926.": "6e764c11625b", "Classic blues.": "e6e0691be92d", "Smith was called the Empress of the Blues, a singer with a huge voice built to carry over a jazz band.": "d1886e24e66d", "Listen for the cornet answering each of her lines, which is call and response, and the A A B pattern of the lyrics, where each first line is sung twice.": "55a71afde3ba", "Now playing: Lost Your Head Blues, by Bessie Smith.": "a5bd63584588", "Three O'Clock Blues, by B.B. King, 1951.": "dfcd74c937f5", "Urban blues.": "b7377b5f28f6", "King was a Mississippi born guitarist whose expressive solo style influenced Eric Clapton, Jimi Hendrix, and many other rock guitarists.": "da299d94219c", "Listen for his guitar fills answering and imitating his singing, bending strings to reach blue notes, over soft, sustained saxophone chords.": "dd6b4a27a801", "Now playing: Three O'Clock Blues, by B.B. King.": "ed6fe0fc3739", "Hoochie Coochie Man, by Muddy Waters, 1954, written by Willie Dixon.": "2464526d5688", "Chicago blues.": "c63032102038", "Waters was a Mississippi slide guitarist who went electric in Chicago and recorded for Chess Records.": "0292d5047095", "Listen for the stop time riff, where the band plays a short pattern and then stops so his voice is alone, and for the extra long sixteen bar choruses.": "b4eeb74133b6", "Now playing: Hoochie Coochie Man, by Muddy Waters.": "fa44fd427a63", "Bo Diddley, by Bo Diddley, 1955.": "368f42c3164e", "Rhythm and blues, and early rock.": "8fb6509b70ec", "Bo Diddley was a Chicago guitarist who called himself a rhythm fanatic and played the guitar like a drum.": "01bfcc91f322", "Listen for the Bo Diddley beat, repeated on guitar, drums, and maracas the whole time over a single chord, with no chord changes at all.": "1587d3759595", "Now playing: Bo Diddley, by Bo Diddley.": "805a6baace2c", "How Far Am I from Canaan, by the Soul Stirrers, with Sam Cooke singing lead, 1952.": "eadc62804124", "Black gospel.": "7293f1643a0b", "The Soul Stirrers were an influential Texas gospel group, and Cooke later became a soul and pop star.": "0feb4114f03e", "Listen for Cooke's melismas, sliding around notes before landing on them, and for the moment the tempo doubles halfway through.": "22fc4ba203f1", "Now playing: How Far Am I from Canaan?, by The Soul Stirrers.": "9c4704bc93b3", "Oh Happy Day, by the Edwin Hawkins Singers, 1969.": "6f2867e7e392", "Gospel, in a large choir style with a rock beat.": "009e297e49e1", "Edwin Hawkins was an Oakland pianist and arranger whose choir took gospel onto the pop charts.": "532592bd7abe", "Listen for soloist Dorothy Morrison's melismas against the choir's plainer answers, and the clapping on the backbeat.": "832582bfee69", "Now playing: Oh Happy Day, by The Edwin Hawkins Singers.": "f649d4c7c076", "Crying in the Chapel, by the Orioles, 1953.": "906cb3ec65d4", "Doo-wop.": "c4a89f1ca1a5", "The Orioles were a Baltimore vocal group, and this cover of a country song was one of the first African American hits on the pop charts.": "c610758737f6", "Listen for Sonny Til's gospel style turns in the lead, over the group's soft ahs and a shuffle beat with a strong backbeat.": "51f4cbb1bcae", "Now playing: Crying in the Chapel, by The Orioles.": "d61f2451ab45", "Sh-Boom, by the Chords, 1954.": "47ce769f4b83", "The Chords were an African American vocal group from New York, and this is the original version of the song.": "e1d2a58e33b2", "Listen for the solo bass singer on the bridge and the jazzy tenor saxophone solo, so you can compare them with the white cover that comes next.": "12ad6a01a225", "Now playing: Sh-Boom, by The Chords.": "22d699454770", "Sh-Boom, by the Crew-Cuts, 1954.": "c8b21964faff", "A pop cover of the Chords' doo-wop hit.": "b087c16b8e23", "The Crew-Cuts were a white group from Canada, and their version outsold the original, hitting number one for seven weeks.": "02828711941b", "Listen for the full swing band, the kettledrum, and the dramatic pauses, and notice that the bass solo and the sax solo are gone.": "49d44308de40", "Now playing: Sh-Boom, by The Crew-Cuts.": "bd76312e53c9", "There Goes My Baby, by the Drifters, 1959.": "69ba417ccf98", "Rhythm and blues vocal group, with Ben E. King singing lead.": "6aad3b17a2fe", "This was the first rhythm and blues record with a full orchestral string section, produced by Leiber and Stoller.": "3c0eb8bd7dec", "Listen for the swirling violins and booming kettledrums behind the voices, with no backbeat.": "3798ce720c9a", "Now playing: There Goes My Baby, by The Drifters.": "22feeb888c27", "I'll Never Get Out of This World Alive, by Hank Williams, 1952.": "9ed072aec290", "Honky-tonk country.": "2e40a767bacf", "Williams was one of country's greatest songwriters, and this was his last single before he died at twenty nine.": "a35e75585270", "Listen for the steel guitar and fiddles sliding between notes, the two beat bass, and his nasal voice with its little crying hiccups.": "0e16c7f81ee6", "Now playing: I'll Never Get Out of This World Alive, by Hank Williams.": "e42cb78f2543", "I Walk the Line, by Johnny Cash, 1956.": "3f320f720a8e", "Country, from Sun Records, the same label as Elvis.": "0d5a4a51e9d9", "Cash, known as the Man in Black, crossed over from the country charts to the pop charts with this song.": "7c46f1dc823d", "Listen for the muffled boom chicka guitar, made by weaving wax paper through the strings, and the two beat bass.": "38d791902289", "Now playing: I Walk the Line, by Johnny Cash.": "43325bc8d0d2", "I'm Walkin', by Fats Domino, 1956.": "86618a0e0379", "New Orleans rhythm and blues and rock and roll.": "167fd6799607", "Domino was a pianist and singer from New Orleans whose friendly voice was popular with both white and African American audiences.": "1dbd9210fd77", "Listen for the hand claps on the backbeat, the shuffle rhythm, and the growling tenor saxophone solo.": "8286cc982089", "Now playing: I'm Walkin', by Fats Domino.": "0f9d4f23c589", "School Day, by Chuck Berry, 1957.": "b1d0c209e53e", "Rock and roll.": "39c79c1ce5fb", "Berry was a guitarist, singer, and songwriter from Saint Louis, often called the Father of Rock Guitar.": "3cebbb651b2c", "Listen for the breaks at the end of each chorus, where the band stops and he sings alone, and for his guitar answering each vocal line.": "1f07e0d395be", "Now playing: School Day (Ring! Ring! Goes the Bell), by Chuck Berry.": "66f83773ff02", "Long Tall Sally, by Little Richard, 1956.": "6b9db3af1ffa", "Rock and roll, with roots in jump blues and gospel.": "d3a98d2df47f", "Little Richard was a pianist and singer from Macon, Georgia, famous for his wild energy, shrieks, and flamboyant image.": "7b4415f4eef0", "Listen for the stop time, where the band hits single chords and drops out so his voice is alone, and the pounding piano.": "ffd6d65e6ab3", "Now playing: Long Tall Sally, by Little Richard.": "778e4dc0bf39", "Shake, Rattle and Roll, by Joe Turner, 1954.": "a8369b47bbb5", "Jump blues, or rhythm and blues.": "1582bedcea5d", "Turner was a blues shouter from Kansas City who sang over honking saxophones.": "1d75a7123c7a", "Listen for the boogie woogie piano, the hand claps on the backbeat, and the jazzy baritone sax solo, so you can compare it with Bill Haley's cover next.": "ea8d7b165ff7", "Now playing: Shake, Rattle and Roll, by Big Joe Turner.": "094afe45417d", "Shake, Rattle and Roll, by Bill Haley and His Comets, 1954.": "87d83c22ee6e", "Rock and roll, from a former western swing bandleader.": "3a5ab3bc03ba", "Haley's version is faster and cleaned up for white radio audiences.": "b702f9844e28", "Listen for the slapped country bass, and the saxophone and guitar playing the riff together instead of a jazz solo.": "29ee9a3ebc71", "Now playing: Shake, Rattle and Roll, by Bill Haley & His Comets.": "2a09890e5823", "Hound Dog, by Willie Mae Big Mama Thornton, 1952, written by Leiber and Stoller.": "bd185b960675", "Rhythm and blues.": "54911c5683fb", "Thornton was a powerful blues singer, and her version was number one on the rhythm and blues charts for seven weeks.": "46590e79afe0", "Listen for her rough, commanding voice, the bluesy guitar bending notes, and the very strong backbeat from claps and drums.": "cff16bfc171b", "Now playing: Hound Dog, by Big Mama Thornton.": "e601d0a72dc1", "Hound Dog, by Elvis Presley, 1956.": "c750d756dcb1", "Elvis's version was number one on the pop charts for eleven weeks, and it changes the meaning of Thornton's song.": "ee393f64e440", "Listen for the faster tempo, the drum hits on the backbeat, the country style guitar solo, and the backup singers holding long chords.": "a7e03637b8be", "Now playing: Hound Dog, by Elvis Presley.": "d453ce27c673", "Burning Love, by Elvis Presley, 1972.": "0d1a1c58e784", "Rock.": "d002bf563d50", "This was Elvis's last top ten hit, from his later career.": "ebe3f373d941", "Listen for the strong backbeat, the mix of straight and swung rhythms, and the backup singers breaking into black gospel style at the end.": "8f060901916a", "Now playing: Burning Love, by Elvis Presley.": "0f4e7bed8272", "Blue Suede Shoes, by Carl Perkins, 1956.": "a7a6e3d85362", "Rockabilly.": "ba8b5cfd8a73", "Perkins was a Tennessee singer, guitarist, and songwriter at Sun Records, and this song was number one on the country charts and number two on pop and rhythm and blues.": "58e803907f69", "Listen for the stop time openings, where his voice is alone except for hits on the beat, the slapped bass, and the drummer playing on the rim.": "0df5f1cc8aaf", "Now playing: Blue Suede Shoes, by Carl Perkins.": "775fc11ba4eb", "Summertime Blues, by Eddie Cochran, 1958.": "aa1de6511eb0", "Cochran was a young rockabilly star who played and sang every part on this record himself, through overdubbing.": "cbb18b4f1be1", "Listen for the instrumental breaks between his lines, and the deep voice he uses to play the boss, his parents, and a congressman.": "47c1789d9472", "Now playing: Summertime Blues, by Eddie Cochran.": "3be0efd63ba6", "Peggy Sue, by Buddy Holly, 1957.": "aedb386e806d", "Holly was a singer, guitarist, and songwriter from Lubbock, Texas, and one of the first rock guitarists to play a solid body electric guitar.": "8f5ce02500b4", "Listen for the nonstop tom tom drumming, the hiccups in his voice, and the guitar solo suddenly bursting out louder.": "5a33a976f73b", "Now playing: Peggy Sue, by Buddy Holly.": "8f29239c0ac7", "From your professor's video list: Jailhouse Rock, by Elvis Presley, 1957, from his movie of the same name.": "18e3e49c7fd0", "Rock and roll, written by Leiber and Stoller.": "622e2688f788", "Listen for the hard, stop time hits that open each verse.": "55d15db9535b", "Now playing: Jailhouse Rock, by Elvis Presley.": "bbd77968b72f", "Blue Suede Shoes, by Elvis Presley, his cover of the Carl Perkins song you just heard.": "a82ecb9f371d", "Listen for how much more he leans into the vocal and the hip shaking stage energy.": "796adaa78ddc", "Now playing: Blue Suede Shoes, by Elvis Presley.": "4152f17e6655", "Later Elvis, from your professor's video list.": "a3346ece74d3", "Listen for how the polished, big production style of his later career differs from the raw Sun and early RCA recordings.": "af0d2214b333", "Now playing: Later Elvis, by Elvis Presley.": "cde35ee41d48", "Little Richard, from your professor's video list, with Long Tall Sally and Tutti Frutti.": "f1d9ef622f6c", "Listen for the pounding piano and his shrieks.": "7ef724ebd0e8", "Now playing: Tutti Frutti, by Little Richard.": "d8508f2860cb", "Jerry Lee Lewis, from your professor's video list.": "6dd0ea21e050", "Rockabilly piano, from the man nicknamed The Killer.": "47475c3a83b2", "Listen for the sweeping glissandos in his right hand, the pounding left hand, and his wild stage antics.": "62635c431318", "Now playing: Whole Lotta Shakin' Goin' On, by Jerry Lee Lewis.": "2ca5a146f889", "That exact recording wasn't available, so here's another song by this artist.": "450ce9653e18", "I couldn't find a preview for this one. Moving on.": "189848ac3821"};
